  "query_preview": "(Today through Sunday)...\nIssued at 228 AM EDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nToday and Tonight...\n\nOn the heels of the first freeze of the season for approximately \nhalf of central Indiana's zones Sunday morning...patchy to \npossibly widespread frost will be the rule early today...while no\nareas drop below 33 degrees. Therefore maintaining Frost Advisory\nfor the entire CWA through 900 AM EDT, although latest trends \nsuggest slightly less organized frost with dwpts so far hanging up\nin upper 30s to near 40F in several, mainly western/southern \ncounties.\n\nBroad and tranquil surface high pressure will facilitate placid \nautumnal conditions for the start of the workweek.  Shift of ridge \naxis out of Indiana to the east will promote modest moderation of \nH850 temperatures that will equate to a 3-5 degree increase in \ntemperatures compared to Sunday.  Mainly clear skies into most of \nthe afternoon will yield to some cirrus crossing from west to east, \nespecially north of I-70, due to a couple very weak mid-level waves \nthat will have no chance of getting any precipitation through the \nvery dry column.  Recent trend in diminishing winds will continue \nwith light flow the rule for at least the next 24 hours...with \noverall variable headings possibly focusing out of the SSE later \ntoday.   \n\nFollowing locally marginal fire weather conditions Sunday...slight \nrebound of dewpoints/higher minimum RH values and light winds today \nwill prevent fire conditions.  High temperatures this afternoon will \nrange from mainly upper 60s to around 70F along the Wabash Valley \nand southwest of Monroe County.\n\nTuesday through Thursday...\n\nZonal ridge across the eastern two-thirds of the CONUS on Tuesday \nwill build northward over the Plains through the mid-week.\nMeanwhile broad and amplified surface high pressure aligned along \nthe eastern half of the continent will gradually shift its center \nfrom southwestern Quebec back into the Great Lakes by Thursday. This \nwill translate to an unseasonably weak surface gradient over central \nIndiana under occasional cirrus.  Wider diurnal temperature ranges \nwill be generally 25-30 degrees...with any overall trend in readings \nmodest and most likely moderating as the building upper ridge to the \nwest allows T850 values to reach 10 degrees Celsius by Thursday.\n\nSurface ridge axis aligned southwestward into East Texas will block \nany organized return of Gulf moisture.  Column moisture will be \nlimited to precipitable water values of 0.90 inches or less, \nmaintaining generally mostly clear skies and surface dewpoints in \nthe 40s to around 50F.\n\nFriday through Sunday...\n\nDry and placid Mediterranean-like conditions to continue under \nstacked high pressure late this week as amplified upper ridge glides \nslowly from the eastern Plains to the Ohio Valley.\n\nEarly winter storm tracking near Hudson Bay later in the weekend \nshould drag the tail of its cold/stationary front across the Midwest \nby Sunday.  However, low confidence in return of any noteworthy \nmoisture ahead of this boundary with stubborn and huge southeastern \nridge blocking/displacing any Gulf moisture well W/NW of Indiana... \nwith only scattered mid/high clouds expected.  Perhaps more notable \nwill be return of light breezes that will veer from easterly Friday \nto southwest by the end of the weekend.  Surprising quiescence in \nfurther temperature trends as potent mid-level vort supporting the \nCanadian system likely suppresses central US ridge somewhat...with \nreadings most likely maintaining range from 40s to 70-75F.\n\nThe normal max/min through the forecast period at Indianapolis is \n63/43. Temperatures across central Indiana will range from \nseasonably cool early mornings to slightly above to above normal \nafternoons.",
